course: dictionary
lesson: 6118
title: Tiny words, big clues
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words describe things you cannot see with your eyes alone. «Most life on Earth is too small to see with our eyes, and scientists still discover thousands of new microscopic kinds every year.» The words too small to see with our eyes are the clue. Microscopic means so small that you need a microscope to see it.
explain: Context also helps when a word is used as a joke. «A microscopic slice of cake» does not mean a slice you need a microscope to see. Someone is joking that the slice is tiny. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then swap your meaning into the sentence. Tiny fits, so you have it.
explain: Look at the sentences around the word, not only the word itself. «For billions of years, all life on Earth was microscopic.» The clues are billions of years and all life, so microscopic describes the very first, tiniest living things. Check your meaning by reading the whole sentence again.
fact: Microscopic means so small that you need a microscope to see it.
fact: In science, microscopic describes germs, bacteria, cells and plankton.
fact: In everyday talk, «a microscopic slice of cake» is a joke for a tiny piece.
fact: «Microscopic life» means living things too small to see without a microscope.
fact: Some clean-looking hands still carry microscopic germs, so washing them properly matters.
exercise: «Most life on Earth is too small to see with our eyes.» What does microscopic mean? | Shiny and smooth | *So small you need a microscope to see it | Far away in space | Very big and heavy
exercise: «A microscopic slice of cake was all he left.» What is the joke here? | A cake made by a microscope | *The slice of cake was tiny | A huge cake for a party | A cake with no sugar
exercise: Which of these can only be seen with a microscope? | A mountain | A horse | *A bacterium | A cloud
exercise: Scientists found «microscopic life» in the pond water. What does microscopic life mean? | Plants with big green leaves | *Living things too small to see without a microscope | Life that lives on the moon | Large fish swimming in the pond
exercise: Jayden says microscopic means very, very small. Is he right? | No, it means very large | Yes, but only in science lessons | No, it only means something you can see with a microscope and nothing else | *Yes, that is its informal meaning, as in a tiny slice of cake
test: «For billions of years, all life on Earth was microscopic.» What does this tell you about the first life on Earth? | It was made of metal | It lived only on land | *It was too small to see without a microscope | It was very big
test: Ella says «microscopic» is a long word, so it must mean something huge. What is the best answer? | No, it means very fast | Yes, because it is in a science lesson | *No, it means very small, and the word micro means small | Yes, long words always mean big things
exam: «For billions of years, all life on Earth was microscopic.» What does this sentence tell you? | *Early life was too small to see without a microscope | Early life was made of metal | Early life lived only on land | Early life was very big
